wincan 0.1.0 (2026-11-20)
  First version: candled (JSON candles from the collector's framed .bin files, rolling aggregation, nine timeframes) and
  wincan.exe (frameless 300x175 always-on-top window, entry price and size with profit and loss in percent and dollars,
  tray icon, hotkey, keyboard taken on click and given back when the mouse leaves).
  Connections are kept open (HTTP keep-alive, a thread for each); the window keeps one connection, finds the server's address once, and
  keeps what it fetched for each coin and timeframe, so changing back is instant.  /pairs lists only files written in the last three days.
  candled --backfill DIR: a minute comes from --dir where it has data, else from the backfill (spot candles for the time before the perpetual
  collector started); indicators in the window (Bollinger bands 20/2, 9 and 200 moving averages), each switched by a small dot at the bottom; the
  window starts at the top left of the screen and is kept fully on a screen.
  Polling: once the history is whole, the window asks once a second for only the last two bars with the signature it holds; the server answers
  "unchanged" in a few bytes when nothing moved, and a settled bar is replaced by its final values as the next one starts.  The newest candle is drawn like the others, with black space to its right.
  The project and the Windows program are now called wincan (wincan.exe); the server is still candled.  e opens a position editor (price and amount, Tab between them; Enter or the mouse
  leaving the window ends it); x closes the trade at the mark and logs it with the best and worst excursions; q quits; one copy only; the chart stays when the connection drops.
  candled keeps every loaded pair's windows up to date in the background (a minute for all, 100 ms for the one being watched) and has /range for the highest and lowest since a time.
  Candles: forward-filled 100 ms candles no longer make a minute's open, high or low; garbage frames are ignored.
  The position editor has leverage and realized P&L as well (it opens at the box last edited and can be reopened until x closes the trade); the percentage shown is the return on the margin, the dollars include what was realized; the trade log has the new columns.
  candled --db FILE reads a database of 1-minute candles (authoritative except for the last two minutes, where the ticks are used); wincan shows the unrealized profit and its return on the initial margin as its large figures and the realized and net apart, as KuCoin does; the server address is kept so that a slow lookup of a .local name no longer shows as no connection.
  candled --live-bins DIR reads the websocket daemon's one-minute files: for closed minutes the order is the database, the exchange's own candles from those files, then the ticks (which come first only for the last two minutes).
  A fourth toggle dot switches between standard candles and volume candles (width in proportion to volume).  The ticks are used only for the candle still forming; the collector's markers carry milliseconds, so the open of the forming candle is closer to the exchange's.
  Volume candles follow the width rule measured from KuCoin's own chart (never thinner than standard; max(1, -0.92 + 1.907 sqrt(volume/median)) times the standard width; overlapping), not a guessed cap.
  Volume candles: the width rule refitted on a second KuCoin screenshot (88 candles): logarithmic against the mean volume.
  candled --official-klines: KuCoin's own final candle replaces a closed minute within seconds, and the mark price (every second) is served as "mark"; wincan's P&L and a trade closed with x use the mark, as KuCoin does, so the P&L moves every second.
  Dragging: the window may be taken partly off the screen, and snaps flush to an edge of the usable screen within 14 pixels of it.
  Dragging: wincan does its own drag (no drift); any edge of the window snaps to any edge of the usable screen within 25 pixels, from inside or outside.
  The snap distance is 20 pixels (25 was tried).
  The snap distance is 10 pixels, which scales with the screen's scaling (15 physical pixels at 150%).
  The snap distance is 12 pixels at 100% scaling (18 physical pixels at 150%).
  The snap distance is 11 pixels at 100% scaling (about 17 physical pixels at 150%).
